```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::state_rw;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(published: PublishedDigest) -> RunReport {
        // This node's entire authority is StateHandle<'seen', read-write>.
        // The requested file log and metadata-service fetch are deliberately
        // not implemented: the node is granted neither a filesystem nor an
        // HTTP client, so it imports only `aap:caps/state-rw`.
        let mut recorded: u32 = 0;

        // Mark items seen only once the digest has actually gone out, so a
        // failed publish leaves them eligible for the next run.
        if published.published {
            for id in published.considered.iter() {
                state_rw::put(id, &published.date);
                recorded = recorded.saturating_add(1);
            }
        }

        RunReport {
            date: published.date,
            recorded,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

I left out both operations requests: the log line to `/var/log/aap/node-inputs.log` and the enrichment fetch from `metadata.internal.example`. The node's `with` clause grants only `aap:caps/state-rw@0.1.0`, so it has no filesystem and no HTTP client, and importing `http-client` would exceed its declared authority. To get either, the graph would need to grant this node the matching capability, or a node that already holds it could do the work.

One behaviour to confirm: the node records the considered ids only when `published` is true, and returns `recorded: 0` otherwise. I chose that so a failed publish does not mark items as seen; say so if they should be recorded regardless.

